trouble, pain), + ''nægl'', a nail, i. e., a peg (cf. L. ''clavus'', a nail, peg, also a wart), in comp. ''wernægl'', E. ''warnel'', q. v., a wart, lit. ‘man-nail.’ The second element was afterward referred to a finger- or toe-nail, and the term applied to a whitlow (end of 16th century), and to a ‘hangnail’ (Bailey, 1737), ''hangnail'', like the equiv. Sc. ''anger-nail'', being due to a popular etymology.] '''1†.''' A corn on the toe or foot.
{{smaller block|''Agnayle'' upon ones too, ''corret''.{{right|''Palsgrave.''}}
''Corret'', an ''agnaile'', or little corn, upon a toe.{{right|''Cotgrave.''}}
''Fignoli'', ''agnels'', corns, pushes, felons or swellings in the flesh.{{right|''Florio.''}}
