'''agnostic''' (ag-nos′tik), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< Gr. ἄγνωστος, unknowing, unknown, unknowable, < ἀ- priv., not, + γνωστός, later form of γνωτός, known, to be known (cf. γνωστικός, good at knowing), verbal adj. of γι-γνώ-σκ-ειν, know, = L. *''gno-sc-ere'', ''no-sc-ere'' = E. ''know'': see ''a-''<sup>18</sup> and ''gnostic.'' The word ''agnostic'' was “suggested by Prof. Huxley . . . in 1869. . . . He took it from St. Paul’s mention of the altar to ‘the Unknown God’ [ἀγνώστῳ θεῷ, Acts xvii. 23]. R. H. Hutton, in letter, . . . 1881.” N. E. D.] '''I.''' ''n.'' One of a class of thinkers who disclaim any knowledge of God or of the ultimate nature of things. {{smaller|They hold that human knowledge is limited to experience, and that since the absolute and unconditioned, if it exists at all, cannot fall within experience, we have no right to assert anything whatever with regard to it.}}
{{smaller block|I only said I invented the word ''agnostic.''
{{right|''Huxley'', London Academy, Nov. 24, 1883.}}}}
{{smaller block|While the old Atheist sheltered his vice behind a rampart of unbelief where no appeals could reach him, the new ''Agnostic'' honestly maintains that his opinions are the very best foundations of virtue.
{{right|''F. P. Cobbe'', Peak in Darien, p. 3.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Pertaining to the agnostics or their doctrines; expressing ignorance or unknowableness.
{{smaller block|That bold thinker in the third century, Clement of Alexandria, declares . . . that the process of theology is, with regard to its doctrine of God, negative and ''agnostic'', always “setting forth what God is not, rather than what he is.”
{{right|''Pop. Sci. Mo.'', XXV. 79.}}}}

'''agnostically''' (ag-nos′ti-kal-i), ''adv.'' In an agnostic manner; from an agnostic point of view; with a tendency or inclination to agnosticism; as an agnostic.
